  'tj.juReady':(f,b)=>'A Jet Upper on its own ('+f+' ★ '+b+'+RP) is judged too — the fastest is f 1f · n 1f · b and RP on one frame (lands '+(TJ.JU_F+2)+'f after the f).',
  'tj.sixCount':f=>f+'f cancels', 'tj.counts':(knee,ju,fast)=>'Knees landed '+knee+' · Jet Uppers alone '+ju+' (fastest '+fast+')',
  'tj.recent':'Recent taunts', 'tj.cancelCol':'Cancel', 'tj.moveCol':'Follow-up → lands', 'tj.help':'How the Taunt Jet Upper is judged',
  'tj.note':(knee,cancel,end,loss,adv,last,ju,fmax,nmax,bearly)=>`Measured in TEKKEN 8 Ver.3.02.02 practice mode (keyboard macro input, checked with the in-game input history and combo counter). The taunt press is 1f · knee ${knee}f (unblockable) · the first input up to ${cancel}f cancels, on ${knee}f or earlier there is no knee · ${cancel+1}~${end}f inputs are ignored (left uncancelled the taunt is −${loss} even after the knee) · the knee gives +${adv}, so only a follow-up landing by ${last}f combos (follow-up startups as the game displays them). Pressing the taunt again within ${knee}f (double taunt) keeps the first press's frames. The Jet Upper is f → n → b+2 (${ju}f startup); alone it was confirmed with f 1~${fmax}f · n 1~${nmax}f · b up to ${bearly}f before RP. Without the n it is b+2 (17f), with RP before b it is 2 (10f). Every input is read per 60Hz cell as its last direction plus that cell's buttons (one direction a frame, as in the game). An f held from before the taunt is not counted as the command (not measured). The site judges the input; it does not guarantee a hit.`,
